    "summary": "Sun‑filled set with killer Dark Star, Here Comes Sunshine and tight China>Rider",
    "blurb": "The show bursts open with a punchy Bertha and rolls through bright numbers like Box of Rain and Jack Straw. Highlights include an inspired Here Comes Sunshine, a soaring China Cat Sunflower → I Know You Rider segue, and a deep Dark Star/Eyes of the World jam. Recording quality is solid on the Miller SBD, making it the go‑to source.",
    "review": "On March 21, 1973 at Utica Memorial Auditorium the Dead delivered one of their most underrated early‑'70 performances. The first set rides a high‑energy wave from a killer Bertha opener through country‑flavored gems such as Me And My Uncle and Wave That Flag, then lifts into bright, melodic moments like Here Comes Sunshine—where Jerry’s lead lines sparkle and Bob’s guitar shines—and the classic China Cat Sunflower → I Know You Rider transition that feels effortless. The second set builds a narrative of love and longing with Greatest Story Ever Told and They Love Each Other before diving head‑first into a monumental Dark Star that seamlessly segues into an expansive Eyes Of The World, showcasing Bill’s propulsive drumming and Keith’s soaring keys. The night closes with heartfelt renditions of Wharf Rat, Sugar Magnolia and Casey Jones, leaving the audience buzzing. Among the available tapes, the Miller soundboard (gd73-03-21.sbd.miller.29263.sbeok.flac) offers the clearest mix—despite a faint hum and a clipped start to Bertha—making it the definitive listening experience for this gem of a show.",
